   "user": "Oz",
   "message": "this video was way deeper than i thought!\n\nproblem with nonlinear narratives is that you usually have to create hundreds of branching paths, but if you design it where the paths dont matter then you can design a base narrative and then cut it up into swappable parts so readers get many narratives from one\n\nit means it's fractal, each meta segment could be further broken down\n\na silly example i think would be narrating a \"Day in the Life\" but in a way where waking up and going to bed doesn't need to happen in order. if it does then it tells the story in one day, otherwise it tells it in 2 (or 3 maybe depending how bedtime and wakeup time line up)\n\nthen the details in between dont matter but bc of how they are told they create wildly different stories bc of the framing\n\nkinda hard to think about but feels important to keep in mind!",
   "timestamp": "2026-08-10T23:24:43.455000+00:00",
